"pre-run state: item_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=0\njournal mode: wal\n\n=== CONCURRENT HISTORY ===\n  time (s)  transaction  op         detail\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  0.000953  txn-1        BEGIN      deferred transaction started\n  0.001121  txn-2        BEGIN      deferred transaction started\n  0.001363  txn-1        READ       quantity=10 reserved=0 available=10\n  0.002393  txn-2        READ       quantity=10 reserved=0 available=10\n  0.002409  txn-2        BARRIER    both transactions open and holding their snapshot\n  0.002432  txn-1        BARRIER    both transactions open and holding their snapshot\n  0.202642  txn-1        WRITE      UPDATE reserved 0 -> 6 (based on snapshot available=10)\n  0.202671  txn-2        ABORT      COMMIT rejected (stale snapshot): database is locked; rolled back, no units reserved\n  0.211845  txn-1        COMMIT     committed reserved=6\n  0.219564  system       FINAL-STATE item_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=6\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n=== SERIALIZABILITY ANALYSIS ===\n\nTransaction outcomes:\n  txn-1: COMMITTED\n  txn-2: ABORTED (write rejected, no committed effects)\n  -> aborted/rolled-back transactions are logically erased; the dependency graph is built over the committed set ['txn-1'] only.\n\nDependency graph (serialization graph, committed txns):\n  nodes: txn-1\n  edges: (none)\n\nAnomaly checks:\n  lost update : NO — both transactions wrote inventory[item_id=1] from the same snapshot, so the writes conflict; only one committed (first-committer-wins aborted the loser). No committed update was overwritten.\n  write skew  : NO — both transactions' write sets are the same row (inventory[item_id=1]), not disjoint, and they could not both commit; write skew requires two committed transactions with disjoint writes validated against overlapping reads.\n\nVERDICT: dependency graph is ACYCLIC -> the schedule IS conflict-serializable.\nEquivalent serial order of committed transactions: txn-1\n  (txn-2 was aborted/rolled back; its reads observed the pre-write snapshot, consistent with serializing it before txn-1. The committed projection alone is equivalent to the serial history [txn-1].)\n\nWhat snapshot isolation prevented:\n  txn-1 and txn-2 both read inventory[item_id=1] at reserved=0 and both attempted to write it.\n  Had both writes committed, the graph would gain rw anti-dependency edges in both directions (txn-2 -> txn-1 and txn-1 -> txn-2: each read before the other wrote) plus a ww edge — a cycle, i.e. the classic LOST UPDATE.\n  First-committer-wins rejected txn-2's COMMIT (stale snapshot), so the cycle never formed and the committed schedule remains serializable.\n\n=== FINAL COMMITTED STATE ===\nitem_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=6"